<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamaudun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamaudun is the royal mausoleum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sho-dynasty">Second Shō dynasty</a> of Ryukyu at Shuri on Okinawa, built in 1501 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shin">Shō Shin</a> to receive the remains of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-en">Shō En</a>. Cut into the rock and built of stone in imitation of a palace, it held the remains of later kings and queens. Damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a> and since restored, it is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamaudun.jpg" alt="Tamaudun" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%89%E9%99%B5_Tamaudun_Royal_Mausoleum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
